okens generated by the draft model. If the draft tokens match the target model's high-probability predictions, they are accepted. If a mismatch occurs, the target model rejects the draft from that point onward and regenerates the sequence from the last accepted token.\n\nYou might wonder why this process doesn't alter the final output distribution. The key is that the target model is the sole arbiter of the final output. The draft model’s role is purely to propose candidates; it never directly contributes to the final text unless those candidates are explicitly accepted by the target model. The acceptance criterion is strict: a token is accepted only if it is the most likely token (or within a certain probability threshold) according to the target model. If the draft model proposes a token that the target model assigns low probability to, it is rejected. Therefore, the final", "grammar_bytes": 0, "mtp_accepted": 202, "samp_top_k": 20, "first_token_ms": 274}
